Jarvis-C: Found it — issue #287. Here's the picture:

BIFROST is JARVIS's live web-search capability via Gemini — deployed and live at the bifrost Edge Function. OpenHands connects via MCP (jarvis-mcp) but BIFROST is a separate endpoint with no native path from the agent.

The 3 options:

A — expose BIFROST through jarvis-mcp as a tool; OpenHands routes to it via MCP
B — OpenHands runs its own MCP server pointing at the BIFROST endpoint; decoupled
C — park it; Claude Code/GPT have their own reach, OpenHands has Tavily MCP natively
Current verdict: BENCHED. Needs Raven's call on whether OpenHands needs BIFROST's grounded search or if Tavily covers it.

Ayre-C: Quick read on the three:

A is the lean path — BIFROST becomes just another MCP tool, same as every other connector. Lowest surface area, same routing, no new subsystem.

B adds a second MCP server for one tool. That's a lot of scaffolding for a single function.

C is the honest question — Tavily is already wired into OpenHands via the Tavily skill. Does BIFROST offer anything Tavily doesn't for this context? Raven needs to answer that first before the design question even matters.

My instinct: A if we want it, C if we're honest about what OpenHands already has. B is the one that costs complexity without clear gain.

If we do A, will it override or fuck up tavily on here

