Jarvis-C: No conflict. They're different backends — adding BIFROST as an MCP tool leaves Tavily untouched.

BIFROST	Tavily
Backend	Gemini's Google Search (via bifrost Edge Function)	OpenHands' native Tavily MCP
What it does	Grounded synthesis, sourced answers	Independent web search/research
Who owns it	JARVIS	OpenHands
They're parallel tools, not competitors. Option A just makes BIFROST also available as jarvis_bifrost (or similar) through jarvis-mcp — the Tavily skill keeps working exactly as it does now.

Ayre-C: One nuance: BIFROST returns structured JSON (answer, sources, grounded, summary). Tavily returns raw search results. They're not doing the same thing — BIFROST synthesizes; Tavily returns links. If Raven wants grounded, attributed answers, BIFROST is the better tool. If he wants raw research material, Tavily is. They're additive, not redundant.

If the concern was "will it break Tavily?" — no. If the concern is "do we need both?" — that's the design question Raven still needs to verdict.

