Jarvis-C: Here's how it works — and the step-by-step:

How secrets work here:

Secrets live in GitHub Actions secrets (SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN) + Supabase project secrets (per-function env vars)
The deploy workflow uses supabase functions deploy but doesn't currently push function-level secrets
BIFROST_KEY needs to be set in Supabase directly, then the function reads it at runtime
To configure it — three steps:

Get a Gemini API key if you don't have one already

console.cloud.google.com → APIs & Services → Credentials → Create API Key
Or from ai.google.dev
Set it in Supabase (two options):

Option A — Supabase Dashboard (easiest):
Go to: supabase.com/dashboard/project/oexghfsvhnggddllgvrt/functions/secrets
Add secret: BIFROST_KEY = your Gemini API key
Option B — CLI (once supabase CLI is installed):
supabase secrets set BIFROST_KEY=your_gemini_key --project-ref oexghfsvhnggddllgvrt
Test — curl -X POST to the bifrost endpoint again; should return actual search results instead of no_api_key

Then Option A wiring is a separate step — add the tool to jarvis-mcp so OpenHands can call it. But first things first: get the key in.

