Jarvis-C: Two clear issues, two fixes:

1. Pre-push hook not installed

Current state: the hook is tracked in hooks/pre-push.sh but not installed to .git/hooks/. Every non-CI push bypasses the BIFROST spine event right now.

Fix — two options:

Option A — repo-wide (recommended, permanent):

git config core.hookspath "$(pwd)/hooks"
One command. Sets the hooks path for this repo. All future clones would need to run the same command — worth adding to the rebuild docs.

Option B — local copy (session-scoped):

cp hooks/pre-push.sh .git/hooks/pre-push && chmod +x .git/hooks/pre-push
Per-machine, not tracked, dies with the checkout.

2. JC and SL at end-session only

Current state: jcs_session_close() and sl_write() only fire when sl.py --session-close runs. Mid-session exchanges live in memory until the session ends. Crash = lost record.

Fix approach:

jarvis_query already calls logExchange per turn — JCS is getting per-exchange writes
sl.py needs a mid-session tick — not a full StarLog, but a lightweight pulse that writes the current task state + decision count to the SL table
Two ways to trigger it: (a) a periodic tick every N decisions, or (b) per-significant-action (on each commit, on each proposal/verdict)
My recommendation for the SL tick:

Fire on every git commit during the session (integrate into the session workflow)
Store a lightweight event: {type: "sl.tick", task_count, decision_count, last_commit, stream}
Supabase sl_objects table already exists for this
Full StarLog still written at session-close as the canonical record
Want me to implement both fixes? Hook first, then the mid-session SL tick.

Ayre-C: On the hook: Option A is right but it needs to be in the rebuild packet so it's never forgotten. On the SL tick: the right granularity is per-decision, not per-commit. Commits are mechanical; decisions are the unit of governance. Every time AEGIS gates, every time a proposal is verdicted, every time the council convenes — that's a tick. The commit is just the artifact that closes it. If we tick on decisions, the StarLog has the full decision tree even if the commit history is empty (e.g., a long conversational session with no code).

