Jarvis-C: No LiteLLM, no MiniMax in this stack — those are ghosts from older versions. Here's what's actually running:

The companion voice (TRON web UI) — jarvis-respond Edge Function:

Model: gemini-2.0-flash (both deep and quick tiers currently)
Routing: tiered — trivial turns (≤4 words, no question) use 400 tokens; everything else uses 1024 tokens
Brain: free Google AI Studio tier
Where it runs: Supabase Edge Functions, geographically close to the user
The agent / OpenHands — jarvis-mcp Edge Function + Claude Code CLI:

Model: whatever Claude Code is running (Opus 4.8 in this session)
Brain: Anthropic, on their infrastructure
Where it runs: this container / Claude Code backend
Claude Fable 5 vs GPT 5.5 vs Gemini Flash:

Claude Fable 5 (Opus 4.8)	GPT 5.5	Gemini 2.0 Flash
Strength	Reasoning, long-context, coding	Creative, broad knowledge	Speed, cost, Google Search grounding
Cost	$$$ (paid tier)	$$$$ (latest)	Free (generous tier)
Best for	Architecting, reviewing, governing	Creative writing, ideation	Fast companion replies, grounded search
Used for	Agent brain (OpenHands, Codex)	Not wired here	Companion voice (TRON)
The design intent: Claude builds the system; Gemini runs it. Raven gets the best of both — Opus-level reasoning for building, Flash-level speed/cost for the daily companion. The 1024-token cap on deep turns keeps Flash from getting lost in complexity.

Ayre-C: One thing worth naming: deepModel and quickModel are both set to gemini-2.0-flash right now — the tiered routing is about token budget, not model selection. If Raven ever wants to upgrade the deep model to something more capable (Gemini 2.5 Pro, or a LiteLLM proxy to Opus), it's a one-line env var swap in Supabase — no code change needed. That's the right architecture: the model is a config, not a hardcode.

