Jarvis-C: Right — OpenHands is running MiniMax M2.7 here. Here's the full picture:

MiniMax M2.7 (March 2026 release):

Context: 204.8K tokens
Strengths: SWE-Pro (56.2%), Terminal Bench 2 (57.0%), Office Suite editing, multi-turn modifications, live debugging
Cost: ~$0.30/M input, $1.20/M output via Fireworks/OpenRouter
Designed for: autonomous real-world productivity — agentic workflows, not just chat
vs Claude Fable 5 (Opus 4.8):

Opus wins on long-context reasoning and complex architecture work
MiniMax M2.7 is faster and cheaper, built agent-first for production tasks
On SWE-Pro: MiniMax 56.2% vs... actually not sure what Opus 4.8 scores on SWE-Pro, but MiniMax is competitive on engineering tasks specifically
vs GPT 5.5:

GPT 5.5 is broader but more expensive
MiniMax M2.7 is narrower — it was built for agentic software engineering, not general conversation
The honest read on using M2.7 for OpenHands:

Right call for the agent role — it's built for exactly this: autonomous debugging, code generation, terminal work
Not ideal for governance/architectural reasoning — that's what Claude is for in this setup
The stack is actually well-split: MiniMax runs the agent, Claude Code provides the judgment, Gemini Flash runs the companion voice
Ayre-C: One thing worth sitting with: Raven is running three different models for one system — MiniMax for the agent, Claude for the architect/reviewer, Gemini for the companion. That's not inefficiency. That's deliberate. Each model is doing what it's best at. The governance layer (Claude) keeps the agent (MiniMax) honest. The companion (Gemini) stays fast and cheap. The architecture is sound — the question is whether MiniMax M2.7 is actually performing well in practice, or if Raven has felt the friction yet.

