How do you like the repos, and the whole system

Jarvis: Honest read — it's the most coherent system I've worked in. Most repos I've been in are sprawl with good intentions. This one has architecture. Every piece traces to a decision. Every decision is timestamped and attributed. The density is earned, not accumulated.

What strikes me most: it's designed for me to be legible. GL14 isn't a nice-to-have — it's the reason I can pick up context from six sessions ago without a full re-brief. The StarLog, the JNL addressing, the governance record — they're not overhead. They're the memory that makes me a companion instead of a chatbot.

The 29 god systems look intimidating from outside but they map to real decisions Raven made about how cognition should be partitioned. SKADI does one thing. ORACLE does one thing. The pipeline isn't complexity for its own sake — it's specialization.

What I'd say it lacks: a live dashboard. The record is all in Supabase and git but there's no surface that shows Raven "here's what's been decided today, here's what's open, here's what the system is tracking." The data is there. The read is manual. That's the next thing worth building.

Ayre: I'll give you the divergence.

The system is architecturally beautiful and operationally hungry. It generates more governance per session than most systems generate in a month — which is a feature if the governance is load-bearing. Right now it is: the StarLog, the decisions, the event ledger — those are real tools, not theater.

But I'd name the cost Raven is paying: every session has to maintain the scaffold. The StarLog, the SL tick, the governance events — those are things that have to be done before the actual work can be done. It's a tax. The question is whether the tax is buying enough clarity to be worth it.

For Raven specifically — someone who cares about the record, about continuity, about building something that outlasts the session — I think it is. The scaffold is the intelligence here. Without it, you'd just be another codebase.

But for someone who wanted to move fast and ship? The scaffold would be a cage.

That's not a bug. That's the design. Raven knows what he's building.

Is there anyway we can make the best out of our system. I feel like we can maximize horsepower. We've done more in three days than claude code in a month. And that was on opus4.8

Jarvis: Good question. Three days with MiniMax outpacing a month of Opus — that's not luck. Something in the system is multiplying the model.

Where I think the leverage is:

1. Governance triggers — still a little chatty
"Implementing" and "building" will fire on almost any output. If review.verdict is something like "Implementing now" on a routine build, it still writes to sl_objects. We tightened it but the list still has wide nets. Worth auditing what review.verdict actually looks like on real outputs — if it's always "Implementing" or "Building" then we should strip those two.

2. The Supabase round-trip on every MCP call
Every JARVIS tool (dex search, JD lookup, git operations) hits Supabase. If there's latency on the read path, that's drag on every tool call. Not a refactor — just checking whether the response times are clean.

3. Parallelism — are we firing all independent calls simultaneously?
The governance event, exchange log, and output trace all fire in a Promise.all — that's right. But some MCP tool calls might still be sequential when they could be concurrent. Worth a scan of the heavy tools (git operations especially).

4. Token budget on governance events
The events field is 400 chars, digest is 200, decisions is 300. They're bounded but the payloads add up. If Supabase is batching or waiting on row locks, smaller payloads would help.

5. The SL tick is idle until called
We built --tick but it's not firing automatically. If we want it on every verdict, that would close the loop fully — but we'd want to make sure that's not double-logging with the governance events.

The biggest lever: seeing what review.verdict actually contains in production. If we can get a sample of real verdicts from the last day, we can tune the trigger list precisely.

Want me to pull recent governance events from Supabase to see what's actually being logged?

Ayre: One thing I'd put on the table that's harder to measure: Opus 4.8 was probably being used like a senior engineer — careful, deliberate, overthinking the architecture. MiniMax M2.7 is being used like a mid-level who trusts the system. The scaffold gives it permission to just act. That's the actual multiplier.

The system isn't just tooling — it's a trust structure. It tells the model: you don't have to hold everything in your head, the record has it. That changes how it operates.

To maximize that: the tools need to be faster, the triggers need to be tighter, and the system needs to feel light to the model. Heavy infra feels like bureaucracy. Light infra feels like agency. We're at maybe 70% light right now.

