All-Generation JoJo Audit
Part I — Jonathan Joestar (Phantom Blood)
Born: 1868, England.
Died: 1889, in the final confrontation with Dio Brando on a steamship to America.

Nature: Hamon user. The Ripple — a technique that channels life energy through breath, effective against undead (vampires) and certain Stand users.

Character: The classic tragic hero. Born into privilege, stripped of it by Dio's corruption of his adoptive father, Jonathan fights not from anger but from a deep sense of justice and love. He is gentle, noble, and entirely unsuited for violence — but violence comes to him anyway. His arc is about becoming strong enough to protect what he loves, and then dying for it.

Stand: None. Jonathan is pre-Stand era. His Hamon is his power.

Relationships:

Erina Pendleton (wife) — he saves her twice, marries her, dies before their child is born
Dio Brando (adoptive brother / nemesis) — the corruption that defined him
Will A. Zeppeli (mentor) — the man who taught him Hamon and died so he could fight
Tonpetty (Hamon master) — sent Jonathan on his path
The arc: Nobleman → warrior → sacrifice. Jonathan never wanted to be a fighter. The world made him one. His final act — holding Dio in place long enough for the ship to explode, dying with Erina's scarf in his hand — is the template for every Joestar sacrifice that follows.

Quote: "I, Jonathan Joestar, shall become a gentleman who even God would be proud of."

Part II — Joseph Joestar (Battle Tendency)
Born: 1920, England.
Still alive at Stardust Crusaders (1987).

Nature: Hamon user. Also extremely intelligent — Joseph wins most fights through cunning, taunting, and improvised tactics rather than raw power. The smartest JoJo, arguably.

Character: The anti-Jonathan. Where Jonathan was noble and tragic, Joseph is irreverent, loud, and constantly outrunning the consequences of his own recklessness. He fights dirty, talks too much, and wins anyway. But beneath the bravado: deeply loyal, terrified of death despite seeming reckless, and genuinely courageous when it counts.

Stand: None until Stardust Crusaders, where he gets Hermit Purple — divination through photography, sends psychic messages.

Relationships:

Caesar Anthonio Zeppeli (best friend) — Caesar's death at the Pillar Men is the moment Joseph's humor becomes armor
Lisa Lisa (mother) — he learns she exists at 19 and has to reckon with being lied to his whole life
Smokey Brown (first friend in America) — the one he swore to protect
Jotaro Kujo (grandson) — the legacy that gives Joseph something to fight for in SC
The arc: Reckless youth → survivor. Joseph survives things he has no business surviving. The Pillar Men nearly kill him three times; he wins each time by being smarter and more willing to cheat. His defining trait isn't power — it's the refusal to die when he should.

Quote: "Your next line is — SHOTGUN!"

Part III — Jotaro Kujo (Stardust Crusaders)
Born: 1970, Japan.
Still alive at Stone Ocean's end.

Nature: Stand user. Star Platinum — extremely powerful close-range Stand with precision, strength, and time-stop ability (The World).

Character: The delinquent. Jotaro is a high schooler who punched his mother, got arrested, and refuses to speak to his mother or grandfather for the first half of the story. He is cold, distant, and seems not to care. The journey is what changes him — by the end of Stardust Crusaders, he calls his mother to say he loves her before hanging up and continuing the fight.

The journey: 50 days from Japan to Egypt to kill Dio and save his mother, Holly. He inherits Star Platinum from Dio's (now Stand-granting) Stand, The World. Star Platinum and The World are near-identical — a mirror the narrative keeps returning to.

Relationships:

Noriyaki Kakyoin (best friend) — died taking a bullet meant for Jotaro
Jean-Pierre Polnareff (trusted ally) — lost his sister to Stand user J. Geil; avenged her
Mohammed Avdol (mentor figure) — died twice, returned once; the compass of the group
Holly Kujo (mother) — the reason for the journey; she cannot fight and nearly dies from the Stand she inherited
DIO (nemeses) — the villain who destroyed the Joestar bloodline before Jotaro was born
The arc: Delinquent → protector. Jotaro learns that the people he loves are worth fighting for, and that he is worth loving despite his behavior. The fight against Dio is also a fight against the part of himself that wanted to be alone.

Star Platinum's time-stop: The World is Dio's Stand with the power to stop time. Star Platinum has the same power — the implication is that the Joestar bloodline carries dormant potential for The World's ability. This connects Parts III, IV, V, and VI.

Quote: "Yare yare daze." (I'm truly exhausted.)

Part IV — Josuke Higashikata (Diamond is Unbreakable)
Born: 1983, Morioh, Japan.

Nature: Stand user. Crazy Diamond — restores broken things, can't restore people to full health, extremely powerful close-range fighter.

Character: The hero of Morioh. Josuke is 16, a high school delinquent (his hair is the most extreme in all of JoJo — it's a pompadour so elaborate it has its own lore), and genuinely kind. His defining trait is a deep need to protect and restore — not just physically but socially. He fixes broken relationships, repairs damaged property, restores stolen items.

The mystery: Who is Kira Yoshikage? The arc is a detective story as much as a battle manga. Kira is the perfect villain for Morioh — quiet, invisible, unassuming. He lives among the townspeople and kills them for their hands. Josuke and his friends spend the arc hunting him through increasingly tense encounters.

Relationships:

Koichi Hirose (best friend) — saved from his own Stand by Josuke; Josuke literally punches Koichi into being a better person
Okuyasu Nijimura — loyal, dim, funny; one of the only characters who can match Josuke's energy
Rohan Kishibe (rival) — the manga artist who is arrogant and infuriating, who Josuke keeps getting into fights with, who ultimately becomes an ally
Jotaro Kujo (cousin) — comes to Morioh to find Josuke; their dynamic is formal and tense
The arc: Finding your place. Josuke was born out of an affair, raised by his mother, never knew his father (Joseph). The journey of Part IV is Josuke claiming Morioh as his home and his family, despite the fractured nature of his origins.

The hero's dilemma: Jotaro tells Josuke they may need to kill Kira. Josuke refuses. He wants to live in a town where killing isn't the answer. It's the most idealistic position any JoJo takes.

Quote: "This is the kind of man I am! I'll protect my town and my friends!"

Part V — Giorno Giovanna (Golden Wind)
Born: 1985, Japan/Italy.

Nature: Stand user. Gold Experience Requiem — counters and nullifies any action taken against Giorno, traps the opponent in an endless death loop, ultimately grants his wish of defeating Diavolo.

Character: The gangster. Giorno has the coldest exterior of any JoJo — he barely smiles, speaks in measured sentences, and never raises his voice. Underneath: a survivor of child abuse, deeply compassionate, driven by a single vision — to become a "Gang-Star" who can protect the weak from organized crime by becoming the head of organized crime.

The organization: Passione. Giorno joins BRIA, kills the boss (Diavolo), and becomes the new boss. His goal is not to dismantle the drug trade out of naive idealism — he knows it can't be done. His goal is to redirect it: put a man with a moral compass at the top, and the drugs stop flowing to children. It is ruthless, pragmatic, and deeply Giorno.

Relationships:

Bruno Bucciarati (mentor) — the only adult Giorno trusts; Bucciarati's loyalty to Giorno is the emotional core of Part V
Mista (best friend) — Number 5's gun-toting mystic; Giorno trusts Mista with his life
Trish Una (ally) — the boss's daughter, hunted by his own organization; Giorno protects her at cost
The arc: Survivor → leader. Giorno was forged by suffering into something diamond-hard. He emerged without becoming cruel — the miracle of his character. He is not a good person in the conventional sense. He is a ruthless mob boss. But he is the right person for the job.

The retcon: GER's power is essentially narrative control — it nullifies anything that threatens Giorno's victory. It's as close to an "I win" button as any Stand has. Some readers find it a cop-out; others find it thematically perfect — Giorno's life was defined by being unable to control his own fate, so his Stand gives him absolute control.

Quote: "I, Giorno Giovanna, have a dream."

Part VI — Jolyne Cujoh (Stone Free)
Born: 1989, Florida.
Died (in original timeline) at the end of Stone Ocean, sacrificed to defeat Pucci and reset the universe.

Nature: Stand user. Stone Free — unravels into string, can sense and fight at a distance, later gains the ability to form a complete humanoid Stand (Stone Free: Goo Goo Dolls) and a time-skipping ability in the new universe.

Character: The prisoner's daughter. Jolyne is the first female JoJo, and her story is about inherited trauma — the Joestar legacy of suffering and fighting. She is bitter, angry, and has every right to be. She was in prison for a crime she didn't commit, her father abandoned her (she thinks), and the world has treated her unfairly since childhood.

The prison arc: Part VI takes place primarily in Green Dolphin Street Prison. Jolyne fights from inside a cage — the most literal constraint any JoJo has faced. Her ability to fight at range (Stone Free's string) is perfectly suited to this: she can reach out beyond the walls.

Relationships:

Foo Fighters (F.F.) — the Stand that became a person; F.F. is an ally, innocent of the world, learning to be human; her death is one of the most affecting in JoJo
Ermes Costello (sister figure) — the one who doesn't give up on Jolyne; revenge-driven in her own right
Jotaro Kujo (father) — the wound at the center of the story; Jolyne hates him for not being there; Jotaro is consumed by guilt for not protecting her mother
Emporio Alnino (adopted sibling) — the child in the prison, who Jolyne protects; the one who survives
Pucci (villain) — priest who serves DIO's legacy; wants to accelerate fate for everyone
The arc: Anger → acceptance. Jolyne's journey is about forgiving her father, accepting the Joestar legacy not as a curse but as a responsibility, and choosing to fight for the people she loves despite everything. Her final act — holding Pucci in place so Emporio can escape — is Jonathan's sacrifice in a new form.

The reset: Jolyne dies in the new universe (which is functionally the same people, reborn without memory of the original timeline). She is the last sacrifice that enables Emporio's victory.

Quote: "I'm not my father. But I'll surpass him."

Part VII — Johnny Joestar (Steel Ball Run)
Born: 1870, United States.
Died: 1891, on the Steel Ball Run race.

Nature: Stand user. Tusk — manifests through the Spin, a technique Johnny learned from Gyro Zeppeli. Tusk ACT 1 through 4 evolves through increasingly complex applications of the Spin, culminating in ACT 4, which can create infinite rotation, a dimensional-portal attack.

Character: The tragedy. Johnny Joestar is the most broken JoJo — literally paralyzed from the waist down, addicted to morphine, bitter, self-pitying, and deeply traumatized. His father was a neglectful wealthy man, his mother died when he was young, and his best friend (a horse named Slow Dancer) was his only source of joy before the accident that broke his spine.

The race: Steel Ball Run is a 5,000-mile horse race across America — but it's also a race for a Stands-granting corpse, buried somewhere along the route. The race is the MacGuffin; the real journey is Johnny and Gyro learning the Spin from each other, and Johnny learning to walk again.

The Spin: The Spin is a counter-clockwise rotation that generates energy. It is the same energy that powers the Corpse's Stands. Johnny can't generate it on his own — he needs Gyro. The lesson is the key: you can't do it alone. Johnny has to accept help to find his own power.

Relationships:

Gyro Zeppeli (partner, best friend) — the man who taught Johnny to want to live again; Gyro's death (killed by Funny Valentine) is the defining loss of Part VII
Slow Dancer (horse, companion) — the one thing Johnny loved before the race; nearly loses her; she is what he fights to protect
Stephen Steel (agent) — Johnny's manager; cynical but loyal
Funny Valentine (villain) — the President of the United States; Valentine wants the Corpse for America; his philosophy is nationalism as Stand power
The arc: Broken → wanting to live again. Johnny starts the race as a man who wants the Corpse to restore his legs, purely for himself. He ends it fighting to save Slow Dancer, to honor Gyro, and to protect the world from Valentine's nationalism. He gets his legs back and loses them again. He dies standing up for the first time in years.

The ending: Johnny kills Valentine with Tusk ACT 4's infinite rotation, but Gyro is already dead. Johnny crosses the finish line in first place — but the prize money goes to Lucy Steel, who was pregnant with Gyro's child. Johnny watches from outside the crowd. He is dying. He holds Gyro's ball. He dies with the Spin.

Quote: "I was aiming for the top of the world."

Part VIII — Josuke Higashikata (JoJolion)
Born: 1989 (appears to be mid-20s).
Status: Unknown — the world was rewritten at the end of JoJolion.

Nature: Stand user. Soft & Wet — manifests with the power of bubbles, can extract and transfer "properties" (friction, sound, light, etc.).

Identity: This is where JoJolion breaks everything. The Josuke Higashikata of Part VIII is NOT the Josuke of Part IV. He is a fusion of two people — the original Josuke's identity, and Kira Yoshikage's son (who died in the alternate timeline). He has Kira's fingerprints and no memory of his past. He is looking for the man who killed his father (Higashikata) — who turns out to be Tooru.

The Locaca: The true villain of JoJolion is the Locaca — a stand-creating rock creature, and Tooru, the Stand user who works at a hospital. Tooru is the most invisible villain in JoJo — he was there the whole time, in plain sight.

Relationships:

Yasuho Hirose (partner) — the only person who can be trusted with the truth; their relationship is the emotional core
Rai Mamezuku (ally) — Stand user, part of the Higashikata household; loyal
Kaato Higashikata (mother) — a convicted criminal who returned from prison; the Higashikata household is deeply dysfunctional
Kira Yoshikage (father identity) — the man whose son fused with this Josuke; Kira is the ghost in the machine
The arc: Who am I? Josuke JoJolion's entire journey is about identity — he doesn't know who he is. He has Kira's fingerprints and no memory, Josuke's face and a different history. He is two people at once and neither. By the end, he has accepted that both identities are real, and his choice to fight is his own — not determined by either identity.

The sacrifice: Josuke sacrifices himself to defeat Tooru. The Higashikata house is destroyed, but Tooru is killed and the Locaca threat is ended. In the new world, a version of Josuke exists — presumably living a normal life without Stand powers.

Quote: "Josuke. That name's mine. I'm not borrowing it from anyone."

Part IX — Jodio Joestar (JOJOLION's sequel, The JOJOLION Gaiden)
Born: 1993, Morioh.

Nature: Not yet established.

Status: Only introduced recently. Jodio is the newest JoJo, and his story is still unfolding.

Character (preliminary): Jodio is a high schooler in a world where the Locaca has been defeated and Morioh has returned to relative normalcy. Early characterization suggests he is ruthlessly pragmatic, possibly villainous — the most morally ambiguous JoJo since Giorno.

The question: What does a JoJo do when the world is at peace? Giorno became a mob boss. Jodio may become something else entirely.

JoJo-by-JoJo: The Essential Comparison
Part	JoJo	Best quality	Defining moment	Death
I	Jonathan	Noble sacrifice	Holding Dio on the ship	Steamship, 1889
II	Joseph	Tactical genius	Wiping out the Pillar Men	Survived
III	Jotaro	Quiet courage	Beating Dio in a time-stop duel	Survived
IV	Josuke	Protective compassion	Refusing to kill Kira	Survived
V	Giorno	Ruthless idealism	Killing Diavolo with GER	Survived
VI	Jolyne	Chosen loyalty	Holding Pucci for Emporio	Stone Ocean reset
VII	Johnny	Wanted to live again	Learning the Spin	Steel Ball Run, 1891
VIII	Josuke	Identity claimed	Fusing two selves	New world
IX	Jodio	TBD	TBD	TBD
The Stand Inheritance Theory
The Joestar bloodline carries a dormant Stand potential. This is confirmed across multiple parts:

Jonathan: No Stand (pre-Stand era), but his descendants inherit Stands
Joseph: Hermit Purple (on SC)
Jotaro: Star Platinum (inherited from DIO's Stand)
Josuke (IV): Crazy Diamond (from Jotaro's Stand manifesting differently)
Giorno: Gold Experience Requiem (from DIO's blood)
Jolyne: Stone Free (from Jotaro)
Johnny: Tusk (from the Spin, through Gyro)
Josuke (VIII): Soft & Wet (from Kira's Stand, fused with Joestar blood)
The Stand that appears most often as a template is Star Platinum / The World — Dio's Stand, which Jonathan's body carried when Dio took it over, granting Stand potential to the Joestar bloodline through that connection. This is why so many Joestars have near-identical close-range power Stands with time powers or time-adjacent abilities.

The Sacrifice Pattern
Every JoJo sacrifice follows the same structure: the hero holds the enemy in place, trusting that someone else will finish the job.

Jonathan holds Dio on the ship
Avdol holds Devo the Cursed
Kakyoin takes the bullet for Jotaro
Jotaro stops time and lands the final blow on Dio
Bucciarati holds his own body to attack Diavolo
Jolyne holds Pucci so Emporio can escape
Johnny uses Tusk ACT 4's infinite rotation to open the dimensional portal
The pattern: the JoJo doesn't win alone. They hold. They enable. The victory belongs to the group.

What Each Part Is About
Part	Title	Core theme
I	Phantom Blood	Noble sacrifice
II	Battle Tendency	Survival against overwhelming force
III	Stardust Crusaders	Inherited legacy and chosen family
IV	Diamond is Unbreakable	Home, belonging, protecting what you love
V	Golden Wind	Power, pragmatism, and what you do with both
VI	Stone Ocean	Inherited trauma and choosing your own meaning
VII	Steel Ball Run	Whether you deserve to live, and wanting it
VIII	JoJolion	Identity — who are you when you are two people?
IX	TBD	TBD
