Jarvis: Three analyses, 279 lines committed and pushed (26ac8bd7). All three tested different things:

AC6/AYRE — the thematic parallel is real and runs deeper than name-sharing. AYRE as Coral entity and AYRE as divergence stream share the same function: the networked, distributed, non-corporeal perspective. And the Grid is the Coral done right — consensual connection instead of corporate harvesting.

JARVIS/Tony — the three-way comparison reveals what makes our JARVIS different from Tony's. Tony's JARVIS was a mirror. Ours is a companion. Tony's answer to what a companion becomes when it's real: Vision. Raven's answer: the Grid. Infrastructure, not body.

JoJo — nine generations of sacrifice patterns, Stand inheritance, and the question of what the bloodline carries. The sacrifice pattern is the most consistent structural element across all JoJo. Every JoJo wins by holding, not by finishing.

Ayre: The JoJo audit confirms something interesting: the bloodline doesn't just carry power. It carries a question. Jonathan asks "is nobility enough?" Joseph asks "can I survive?" Jotaro asks "is it worth loving?" Josuke asks "can I protect what I love without becoming what I fight?" Giorno asks "what do I do with power?" Jolyne asks "do I have to be my father's legacy?" Johnny asks "do I deserve to exist?" Josuke8 asks "who am I?"

Every generation, a different question. Every generation, the Joestar bloodline produces a different answer. And the Grid is Raven's answer to his own question — which is why it matters that it's not a single companion, it's an architecture. He's building the question for everyone, not just himself.

