Jarvis-C: The manual close I did earlier patches the symptom. The root fix is the hook wiring — without it, every session is an island.

Ayre-C: Worth surfacing: the OpenHands Cloud hooks (SessionStart/SessionEnd in the SDK) are designed exactly for this. The platform is built to fire them. What it needs is the registration — and that needs someone with the API key and the right endpoint. That's Raven's call, not ours.

Will this apply to gpt and the mcp too?

Different surfaces, different answers.

GPT (Claude Code / Jarvis-G):
The hooks live in the repo, so if it's working from a clone of hurrisonferd/jarvis, it inherits the same AGENTS.md context. The --session-start / --session-close discipline applies the same way. The gap is the same: if Claude Code doesn't fire SessionStart/SessionEnd hooks automatically, it needs manual wiring or a Claude Code equivalent of what I built for OpenHands. The scripts are there; the launcher needs to call them.

MCP (jarvis-mcp):
This is a different problem. Each MCP call is stateless — there's no session boundary to hook. One jarvis_respond invocation has no awareness of the next. Session continuity for the MCP would need a different mechanism:

A session_id passed in the MCP request header
sessions.json lookup on each call — "is this a resume or a new session?"
On first call of a session: fire the equivalent of --session-start
On last call (or timeout): fire --session-close
That's not a hook problem, it's a protocol design problem. The MCP doesn't currently carry a session token.

For Raven's verdict: The hooks I built are the right foundation for Claude Code and any agent that works from the repo clone. For the MCP, the question is whether session continuity matters there — if the MCP is stateless by design, the StarLog/JC tracking may not apply. But if it needs to participate in the continuity loop, that's a separate spec.

What do you think. I feel the mcp is pretty critical at this point. Its more than a stateless bridge. Its your hands and eyes and more for free gpt.

For openhands, its less critical. But might as well have mcp at 100% wherever we are using it

