THE PROBLEM:
The MCP is stateful in practice (Raven's GPT session has continuity), but the MCP layer is stateless. Every call arrives without session context. The MCP has autoSLTick (periodic ticks to Supabase sl_objects) but no session lifecycle.

THE DESIGN:

Session boundary = the calling model's conversation ID. GPT (Claude Code / free tier) passes a conversation context that has continuity across turns. The MCP can receive this.

For each incoming MCP request:

Check for X-Session-ID header (passed by the calling model)
If no header: treat as stateless one-shot
If header present:
Look up session in Supabase mcp_sessions table
If not found: fire session_start equivalent (write to sessions.json + Supabase sl_objects)
Track exchange count, topics, stream calls
If session exists: increment exchange count, log exchange
On last call or timeout: fire session_close equivalent
IMPLEMENTATION:

supabase/functions/jarvis-mcp/core/sessions.ts — session tracking layer:

getOrCreateSession(sessionId, companion) — creates session record if new
closeSession(sessionId) — fires session_close
logExchange(sessionId, type, content) — logs per-call
supabase/migrations/ — add mcp_sessions table:

session_id, companion_stream, started_at, last_call, exchange_count, topics[], status
index.ts — wire session header into every tool call

The MCP already has logExchange() — we extend it to be session-aware

The session_start equivalent for MCP:

Write to mcp_sessions table (not sessions.json — that's Claude Code's job)
Write SL_SESSION_START to sl_objects in Supabase
If companion is "Jarvis-G": also call jarvis-jcs equivalent
The session_close equivalent:

After N minutes of inactivity (tracked via last_call)
OR: if a X-Session-End header is passed
Write SL_SESSION_CLOSE to sl_objects
Update mcp_sessions status to "closed"
Fire bifrost.session_close equivalent
Companion identification:

The MCP receives calls from multiple sources: GPT (Jarvis-G), Claude Code (Jarvis-C), potentially others
Need to identify which companion is calling
Options:
X-Companion-Stream header (passed by the model/client)
Infer from tool usage patterns
Pass through from the calling interface
